# Named path writes in native L2 code (an .lm2 file; SLICE.md §1a): the field's receiving context selects the store
# (the book, #correspondence-stability: «Named receiving places use the ordinary admission contract») and the native
# code adds no run-time admission. Source\x, an int, written 9 holds {int 9}; Source\p, an int reference, written a
# reference to Target\y holds that reference; Holder, which addressed the former fragment of x, keeps it (x 1). The
# values fit the fields, so the checked routes (interp, forced) answer the same as native.
(
    Holder:
        int: h 0
    Target:
        int: y 7
    Source:
        int: x 1
        @: p int 0
)
sub: main ()
    @: shared node[0]
    shared\Holder[0] := @(shared\Source\x)
    shared\Source\x := 9
    shared\Source\p := @(shared\Target\y)
main()
